<!--
    GH-829 (kuna): "A variadic call drops an argument the caller forwards
    unchanged in its own register"
    https://github.com/Noelo-Lab/kuna/issues/829

    Source, gcc -O2:
      int pr(const char *f, ...);
      int d2(const char *f, int a) { return pr(f, a); }
      int d3(const char *f, int a) { if (a > 5) return 0; return pr(f, a); }
      int d5(const char *f, int a, int b, int c) { return pr(f, a, b, c); }

    d2 is `endbr64; xor %eax,%eax; jmp pr` and d5 the same: every argument is
    still in the register it arrived in.  The bytes are gcc's, with each jmp
    aimed at the `ret` standing in for pr at 0x100100.

    Stage model: P4 call recovery, active-input-trial scoring.  Upstream's
    AncestorRealistic fails a trial whose value at the call is the function's
    own input, so the esi/edx/ecx trials are inactive and, with nothing active
    after them, dropped.  kuna_varargforward.rs (always on) keeps such a trial at a call
    whose prototype ends in `...` when the calling function declares a
    parameter at that storage and the call is the only reader of it.  d3 also
    compares `a`, so it has another reader and is left alone.

    The authored MSABI `stored` probe at 0x100200 loads/increments EDX,
    stores the value through RCX, then passes the immutable "%i" format in
    RCX. The store is another reader; the format proves the promoted integer
    slot and the same value is passed. Assertion 2 requires two distinct
    return-call lines: the forwarded call and the stored case.

    Without the rule d2 and d5 print `pr(f)`; with it `pr(f,a)` and
    `pr(f,a,b,c)`.
-->
